  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how limb skeletal elements and joints arise during embryonic development. It begins with a body cross-section showing somites and lateral plate mesoderm, then links these tissues to the forming limb bud. The lower inset sequence shows somite differentiation into dermomyotome and then into dermatome, myotome, syndetome, and sclerotome, emphasizing the mesodermal sources that contribute to limb-associated tissues.\n\nIn the limb bud, a central cartilage template forms, surrounded by a perichondrium. A distinct band labeled the joint interzone appears between adjacent cartilage regions, indicating where a synovial joint will develop and split one continuous cartilage model into separate skeletal elements. The cells in this interzone are drawn differently from the cartilage cells, highlighting a specialized tissue zone involved in joint formation.\n\nAt the far right, the developing limb skeleton is organized along the proximal–distal axis into stylopod, zeugopod, and autopod. These correspond to single proximal bones such as the femur or humerus, paired middle bones such as radius/ulna or tibia/fibula, and the more distal wrist/ankle, hand/foot, and digit elements. Overall, the image conveys the developmental progression from mesodermal precursors to segmented limb bones and joints.",
